WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:05.246
- We have six weeks to harvest
a million Christmas trees,

00:00:06.338 --> 00:00:09.505
and that means you've gotta move fast.

00:00:10.674 --> 00:00:12.278
As soon as harvest starts,

00:00:12.278 --> 00:00:14.960
it's time to call in the cavalry.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:17.627
(intense music)

00:00:31.762 --> 00:00:33.302
The state of Oregon is
the largest supplier

00:00:33.302 --> 00:00:35.295
of Christmas trees in the country.

00:00:35.295 --> 00:00:39.462
Here in Oregon, Christmas trees
outnumber people 12 to one.

00:00:40.885 --> 00:00:42.104
Holiday Tree Farms is one of the largest

00:00:42.104 --> 00:00:44.664
Christmas tree farms in the world.

00:00:44.664 --> 00:00:48.211
We have about 8,000 acres
of actively growing trees.

00:00:48.211 --> 00:00:50.917
The only thing we grow is Christmas trees.

00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:52.848
We sell a million Christmas trees a year,

00:00:52.848 --> 00:00:55.158
but you gotta remember, we
harvest them in a very short

00:00:55.158 --> 00:00:56.822
period of time.

00:00:56.822 --> 00:00:59.494
We can cut and process a
Christmas tree and have it loaded

00:00:59.494 --> 00:01:01.837
on a truck in less than 24 hours.

00:01:01.837 --> 00:01:03.750
The steps in harvesting
a Christmas tree start

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:07.236
with cutting each tree
individually with a chainsaw.

00:01:07.236 --> 00:01:10.039
Slingers will pile the trees
and rig them for extraction.

00:01:10.039 --> 00:01:11.936
Next, we bring a helicopter to fly them

00:01:11.936 --> 00:01:13.404
in to waiting trucks.

00:01:13.404 --> 00:01:15.666
Each tree is shaken, baled and loaded,

00:01:15.666 --> 00:01:17.915
and then it's on its way to market.

00:01:17.915 --> 00:01:21.332
(helicopter blades slap)

00:01:27.171 --> 00:01:31.338
Holiday started using
helicopters in the early 1980s.

00:01:33.459 --> 00:01:37.042
They're a lot faster
than doing it by hand.

00:01:39.778 --> 00:01:44.201
Helicopter can harvest
about 1,000 trees an hour.

00:01:44.201 --> 00:01:46.907
We normally run between five
or six helicopters every day,

00:01:46.907 --> 00:01:48.490
sun up to sun down.

00:01:55.633 --> 00:01:56.942
We've been taking care of these trees

00:01:56.942 --> 00:01:59.469
ever since they were very tiny seedlings.

00:01:59.469 --> 00:02:02.257
An average normal fir
is about eight years old

00:02:02.257 --> 00:02:05.454
when you harvest it, and
it's six to seven feet tall.

00:02:05.454 --> 00:02:07.282
Harvest is my favorite time of the year,

00:02:07.282 --> 00:02:09.459
that's when we get to see
the payoff for six, seven,

00:02:09.459 --> 00:02:12.298
eight years worth of work.

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:14.215
For every tree that we harvest,

00:02:14.215 --> 00:02:16.376
we plant another tree in its place.

00:02:16.376 --> 00:02:17.775
So, when we harvest a
million trees we plant

00:02:17.775 --> 00:02:20.124
over a million trees in the spring,

00:02:20.124 --> 00:02:21.707
and it never stops.

00:02:24.184 --> 00:02:25.258
So, what do I think of fake trees

00:02:25.258 --> 00:02:27.171
as a Christmas tree farmer?

00:02:27.171 --> 00:02:29.926
They're our number one
competition, obviously.

00:02:29.926 --> 00:02:32.783
We real tree farmers have
a saying about fake trees.

00:02:32.783 --> 00:02:34.926
Nine years in the house,
and nine million years

00:02:34.926 --> 00:02:38.060
in a landfill, they cannot be recycled.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:40.560
It's Christmas, keep it real.

